            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Andrew D. Hurwitz, the petition argues that the Sixth Amendment right to a jury trial requires a jury to find any fact that could increase a defendant\u2019s sentence, as established in *Apprendi v. New Jersey*."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Andrew D. Hurwitz",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Andrew D. Hurwitz, arizona law permits a death sentence only if the jury\u2019s verdict alone supports life imprisonment; the judge has no discretion to impose death."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Hurwitz",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 19,
              "start": 19
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Hurwitz, the petitioner further contends that while a jury sentencing recommendation is not required, a jury finding of aggravating facts is necessary."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
