              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Andrew D. Hurwitz, the petitioner relies on *Apprendi* to argue that any fact increasing the penalty must be found by a jury."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Hurwitz",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 19,
                "start": 19
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Hurwitz, the petitioner asserts that a jury must find the aggravating facts that make a death sentence possible."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Hurwitz",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 22,
                "start": 22
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Hurwitz, identifying the most culpable homicide defendants for death penalty eligibility requires a narrowing process that isolates aggravating circumstances."
        }
      ],
